  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "1",
  "band": "K-2",
  "focus": "Grade 1 owns descriptive precision and the language of sequence and reasoning. Children move from naming things to describing them exactly (enormous, narrow, shiny), from telling events to ordering them (first, next, finally) and explaining why they happened (because, reason, cause), and from everyday places to the worlds of weather and animal habitats. It adds the first true story vocabulary (setting, solution, adventure) and the first two derived-word patterns, un- and -er. It differs from kindergarten, which stays with concrete, sensory, and feeling words and simple opposites, and from grade 2, which takes on words with several meanings, richer feelings and character traits, comparing language, and simple nonfiction text features.",
  "selection": "Words were chosen for oral-language utility, concept value, and fit with first-grade read-alouds. Sequence and cause words (first, then, because, so, if) are in the second and third units because they unlock how children tell and explain everything else. Describing words are grouped by what they describe (size and surface, movement, weather) so children learn a better word for something they already know. Language-of-instruction words (describe, explain, compare, measure) appear in the first and last units. Morphology is limited to un- and the -er doer suffix, the two patterns first graders can build on their own. Tier 3 words (weather, habitat, measure, length) appear only in three cross-curricular units, never more than two per unit. Deliberately left for kindergarten: simple opposites, positions, and basic feelings. Deliberately left for grade 2: multiple-meaning words, comparing language (similar, contrast), richer character words (generous, stubborn), and nonfiction features.",
  "arc": [
